A||||11||fiero56||How to get stains off of garage floor||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||01-14-2001||05:37 PM||||Our house is going on the market in one month, so I need to get the garage floor cleaned up. It has stains from clutch jobs, suspension replacement, and other messy tasks I have performed. I have one of those heavy floor scrubbing machines that have the big "brush disk" that spins orbitally. I tried using regular old dishwashing soap since it has grease cutting properties, but it only took stuff off the surface. We have a smooth concrete floor in the garage, and it seems many of the stains have soaked in. Whats good at getting those stains out of the concrete? I would prefer something I don't need a ventilator for and won't take my skin off.||||reg||5
Z||000001||fropuff||01-14-2001||06:21 PM||||I'm not sure of a particular brand, but if you go to a place like Menards or Home Depot, there's some cleaners that are made for that type of thing.  They are special ones for certain stains as well.  Might want to use them with that scrubber.  At my work, we used some cleaner that is kinda gritty, smells like Ajax, and changes color when it gets wet.  We threw that stuff on, used the scrubber, rinsed it off, and squeegeed it.  Turned out a lot better.  By the way, it was at Kolar Auto in the service department.  There was the same crap all over the place.  Turned out great.  I'll try to find out what the cleaner was.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||01-14-2001||07:29 PM||||Try something with a chemical known as trisodium phosphate, or TSP, it's one of the active ingredients in "Spic&Span" cleaner.<P>Be very careful though, it's really potent stuff.  You don't want to get the powder or a solution you make with it on your car for example.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||01-14-2001||09:38 PM||||Needs to be ventilated and it will burn your skin, but I use Miuriatic (sp?) acid with great results.. Pour her down, let it get green and bubbly, and wash it away.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||01-14-2001||10:24 PM||||Im with LarryB and JohnnyK.  I work at a hardware store, and we recommend muratic acid for washing concrete.  Definatly ventilate well, and wear gloves especially for use with tough chemicals.  Protect your eyes.  Even when in the bottles, it will eat through the shelf that it is sitting on (steel) after about a year.  stuff must leak out at night or something...<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||01-14-2001||10:34 PM||||I tried some Simple Green and it worked better than dishwashing liquid, but didn't get it all up.<BR>I'm hoping for something that I can hose out of the garage(no drains in the floor, and it has a very slight tilt towards the driveway. I need to make sure whatever I put down isn't going to kill off the grass along the driveway.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||01-15-2001||09:42 AM||||56,<P>A couple of years ago, I bought some driveway cleaner from an auto parts store.  I have never used it... but will happily give it to you if I can find it.  I think I know where it is.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000007||Oreif||01-15-2001||10:56 AM||||Pour some Coca-cola on the stains and let it sit overnight then wash the floor with car wash soap (Turtle wax brand) mixed at 2 parts water 1 part soap. (worked on some trans fluid stains in my garage)||||reg||1
Z||000008||rodmcneill||01-15-2001||11:32 AM||||Diet or regular?||||reg||12
Z||000009||theogre||01-15-2001||02:05 PM||||Try this fisrt. It's allot safer than some of the alternatives and won't alter the look of the concreat finish like some things will.<P>Liquid laundry soap. cut it about 40-50% with water and smear it on the stain. let it sit and work. keep it wet and work it with a brush once in awhile. After an hour or two hose it off. It works even better if the concreat is warm. (If the surface is cold let is sit even longer.) I've lifted some really deep stains with this method. You don't have to worry about neutralizing or anything like that after. (How strong is laundry soap? Many drag strips use it to clean the track.) The laundry soap will actually draw the oil out of the concrete. the key is keeping it wet while it works. (Any soap/detergent needs water. If you use it straight it won't work as well if at all.)<P>If you want to leave it work overnight just cover it with some wet rags. The longer you let the stuff soak the more oil it will draw from the concrete.<P>For any method where you want a warm rinse water source. Get a Y hose. (It has 2 female ends tied to one male.) this will let you hook up to the washing machine taps so you can run warm water. Don't run straight hot water! Most garden hose and accessories aren't made to be used above 100F and can burst. <P>It would also seam like you could use a water bed fitting and hook to a sink. Don't do this either. The water bed adaptors aren't made to take any pressure. They will likely blow out of the tap.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||01-15-2001||03:10 PM||||Good suggestions, Ogre. I will give it a try.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Joe4x4||01-16-2001||07:27 AM||||I heard of the Coca-Cola trick too.<BR>Another trick is to use WD-51 ...fight grease with grease. I also used engine degreaser too that worked pretty good too.<P>Good luck......<P>------------------<BR>Fiero got fire<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/exp.gif">        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/animated_cat.gif"> ||||reg||6
